# Boundary-directed marker rays: case note

A useful ARC pattern has a uniform field, sparse interior marker cells, and thick bars along grid edges. In a demonstrated 29×14 case, sparse 6 markers were changed to 4 and each marker's row was painted 6 from that location rightward up to (but not including) a solid 0 bar at the right edge. Other right-edge-bar rows stayed unchanged, so the mere presence of a bar did not activate a row. A corresponding vertical ray may be expected when a marker aligns with a solid bar on the opposite edge, but verify its direction and exact cells in a demo rather than assuming symmetry.

Validation checklist:
- compare output and input at every coordinate;
- distinguish marker recoloring (6→4) from ray painting (background 8→6);
- include the marker cell only if the output shows it recolored instead of ray-colored;
- stop before the boundary bar and preserve that bar;
- don't infer full edge-bar expansion from isolated changed rows/columns;
- apply only the ray behavior directly supported by examples.

The example also contained some complete horizontal bands adjacent to an opposite-edge bar, but their selection rule was not determined by available evidence. Treat such changes as unresolved rather than generalizing them to all rows.

# task-ebb0e4e4: two-demo right-tail recurrence (unresolved)

This is a distinct instance from the prior eight-demo unresolved mixed-tail run and the accepted task variants. Do not inherit its inferred candidate answers or the accepted variants by task ID alone.

## Exact evidence

Demo 1 (25x18, background 4, pattern 0): only these cells changed to 1 (0-indexed):
- row 2, col 17;
- row 6, cols 6–17;
- row 10, cols 10–17;
- row 15, col 17;
- row 19 was a full 0 row and did not change;
- row 23, cols 12–17.
All other cells stayed the same. Notably the selected rows have a leading 0 run followed by background 4, but the full 0 row is a negative example.

Demo 2 (13x14, background 4, pattern 6): only row 1 cols 12–13 and row 5 cols 10–13 changed from 4 to 1. Both rows contain an initial run of 4, followed by a long 6 run, followed by a right-side 4 tail. The visually similar rows 11–12 were preserved. This supports selecting some right-tail segments after a pattern run, but does not identify why these two rows are selected over preserved lookalikes.

## Test outcome / caution

Three test submissions failed. They variously (a) transformed apparent right tails after the row-6 pattern, (b) recolored prefixes/regions as well as tails, and (c) changed only the rightmost suffix of visible sparse-pattern rows. None is validated. The correct selector and whether continuation is based on a cross-row motif, pattern phase, or a special row structure remain unresolved. Do not reuse these attempted test grids. Before another test attempt, explicitly identify selected and preserved candidate rows in both demos and find a predicate that predicts all changed and unchanged cells; request a targeted demo only if it separates concrete candidates.

## Process lesson

Do not convert a partial visual resemblance from one demo into a test-wide heuristic. After every demo, first extract exact changed cells and negative examples, then reconcile the new pair with all prior pairs before submitting or requesting another demo. In the reviewed conversation, guesses after demo 1 and demo 2 were both speculative and failed; the second demo was not analyzed before another submission. Treat demo cost as a reason to analyze carefully, not as pressure to guess early.
